{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 demands a causal mechanism but its 'Error-Consistency Rate' fails to provide one: it proposes that a panel following a checklist to a wrong answer proves reliability, but it does not establish a causal link showing why consistent errors indicate a reliability gain over intuition. Being consistently wrong per a checklist could be a symptom of a flawed checklist or systematic panel bias, not of improved reliability. The target offers no mechanism for how rejecting an outcome-suggested conclusion actually causes better judgments on average; it merely labels the behavior as 'reliable' without explaining the cause.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 demands a causal mechanism but offers only a metaphor ('lossy compressor') for how the checklist removes bias. It states that non-experts 'operate on the signal without the noise,' but it never specifies the causal process by which following distilled rules produces more reliable outcomes than intuition. The 'Cross-Item Consistency' evidence is a descriptive marker of compliance with a trap item, not a demonstration that the mechanism actually causes improved reliability. There is no causal account of why retaining 'decision-boundary logic' while stripping idiosyncratic bias necessarily yields better average performance.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}